.TH setdate 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
setdate \- set the clock, from the battery-backed one or by hand
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B setdate
[\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-u\fR] [\fB\-w\fR] [\fB\-0\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
FUZIX keeps two clocks: the kernel one, which starts at zero on every
boot, and the DS3231 on the board, which keeps time while the power is
off.
.B setdate
moves the time between them, or takes a new one from you.
.PP
With no options it reads the DS3231 and sets the kernel clock from it,
saying nothing if that works.  If there is no clock to read, it asks you
for the date and the time instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Automatic: read the DS3231 and set the kernel clock, and if that fails
just exit 1 rather than asking.  This is what
.B /etc/rc
uses at boot, where there is nobody to answer.
.TP
.B \-u
Ask, whatever the DS3231 says.  This is how you correct a wrong time.
.TP
.B \-w
The other direction: write the kernel time out to the DS3231, so it
survives the next power off.  Do this after setting the time by hand or
with
.BR ntpdate (1).
.TP
.B \-0
The time you type is UTC rather than local.
.SH ENTERING A TIME
The date is asked for as
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD ,
the time as
.BR HH:MM:SS ,
seconds optional.  An empty line at either prompt leaves that part
alone.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# setdate            set the kernel clock from the DS3231
# setdate -u         type a new date and time
# setdate -w         save the current time to the DS3231
# ntpdate time.cloudflare.com
# setdate -w         ... and keep what it fetched
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
1 if
.B \-a
was given and the DS3231 could not be read, or if
.B \-w
failed; 2 if the kernel refused the new time.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR date (1),
.BR ntpdate (1),
.BR cal (1)
